		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=2753#p2753"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div>1) the 'desired resulting velocity' which includes the restitution is calculated and stored before the solver / impulse iterations start. This is what Bullet does, see<br>2) Another approach is to just scale the resulting impulse after all iterations finish by the restitution factor.<br><br>Jan, are you using one of above 2 methods to handle restitution, or another one? </div></blockquote>I use both  <img class="smilies" src="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/images/smilies/icon_wink.gif" width="15" height="15" alt=":wink:" title="Wink"> <br><br>In fact I implemented one method which is based on Newton's impact law. This method computes first the 'desired resulting velocity' for every contact point and stores it. Afterwards impulses are computed to reach these velocities. Of course you have to do this in a loop since new collision can occur due to the impulses. <br><br>I also implemented a method based on Poisson's hypothesis. It works in the same way but in the beginning the 'desired resulting velocity' is set to zero. In this way I get the impulses of impact. Then I use Poisson's hypothesis to compute the resulting impulses of the collisions. <br><br>Jan<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=818">Jan Bender</a> — Sat Dec 09, 2006 6:54 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
